      "thinking": "I'm tracing the monthly-spend-feature flag: it switches to the monthly average calculation, which is O(n) and cheap, while the lifetime path with its O(n\u00b2) prefix-sum recomputation stays untouched \u2014 so toggling the flag shouldn't be the source of the slowdown.\n\nBut then I notice the cache path in `_the_figure_for`: when cached_summary hits, the figure comes from cache regardless of the flag, meaning the flag doesn't actually bypass caching. Rechecking the docstring, monthly-spend-feature actually maps to use_monthly_statement, which walks purchase history directly \u2014 each of the_biggest, the_smallest, the_mean, and the category/band summaries call purchases_this_month repeatedly, but each call is still linear, so nothing there looks quadratic either. I'm wondering if the deployed commit is older than the one I'm inspecting, which could explain the discrepancy.\n\nStatement_sections gets recomputed by several downstream calls but always over the small monthly statement, so that's not the bottleneck. The p99 spike is confined to accounts with long purchase histories, which makes me suspect something like average_spend_per_item in spend_summary \u2014 that's O(n\u00b2) \u2014 but that's part of the lifetime path, not the monthly one, and it's been live for a while, so turning the flag on (which switches to monthly) shouldn't newly expose it. I think I need to check rollout.py, typical_spend.py, and accounts.py to see if something there ties the flag to the quadratic code path.\n\nOne possibility: Account.purchases might be a lazy property that re-queries the database on every access, so if monthly_statement calls purchases_this_month repeatedly, that could cause repeated expensive DB walks \u2014 worth confirming by reading accounts.py.\n\n",
